oc=['https://docs.kernel.org/mm/overcommit-accounting.html','https://docs.kernel.org/admin-guide/sysctl/vm.html'];cg=['https://docs.kernel.org/admin-guide/cgroup-v2.html'];sm=['https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man5/proc_pid_smaps.5.html'];oom=['https://raw.githubusercontent.com/torvalds/linux/master/mm/oom_kill.c'];ku=['https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/scheduling-eviction/node-pressure-eviction/','https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/configuration/manage-resources-containers/'];re=['https://redis.io/docs/latest/operate/oss_and_stack/management/admin/'];ja=['https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs/specs/man/java.html'];ls=['https://clang.llvm.org/docs/LeakSanitizer.html','https://clang.llvm.org/docs/AddressSanitizer.html'];bc=['https://github.com/iovisor/bcc/blob/master/tools/memleak.py']
def e(a,c,r,s=oc):edit(m,a,c,r,s)
def f(n,k,c,r,s=oc,o=None):qe(q,'mem-in-practice-q'+str(n),k,c,r,s,o)
e('Why malloc almost never fails on Linux','How overcommit changes allocation failure on Linux','Unsupported frequency claim.')
e('1. Linux promises more memory than it has (**overcommit**).','1. Linux can permit commitments beyond available physical backing (**overcommit**), according to policy.','Not all configurations.')
e('2. When the promises come due and cannot be met, it kills something (the **OOM killer**).','2. Severe allocation pressure can cause reclaim, allocation failure or an **OOM kill**, depending on context and policy.','Not every failure kill.',oom)
e('## Overcommit: malloc rarely fails','## Overcommit and delayed failure','Remove unmeasured frequency.')
e('Demand paging means a successful `malloc` or `mmap` only reserves *address space*. Frames are allocated on first touch.','A successful `malloc` or `mmap` need not make every requested byte resident. Existing allocator pages, metadata and population flags can already consume memory; new anonymous backing is often supplied on first touch.','Not all malloc onlyreservation.')
e('| 1 | Always say yes |','| 1 | Skip the overcommit admission check; other limits and allocation failures still apply |','Mode1notallmallocsuccess.')
e('In mode 0, a single request larger than roughly RAM plus swap fails, but many smaller ones succeed regardless of the total.','Mode 0 applies a heuristic, not a guarantee that each request below RAM succeeds. Resource limits, address-space availability, mapping limits, metadata allocation and current memory conditions can still reject requests.','False guaranteed request formula.')
e('In mode 0 every call succeeds: 80 GiB of virtual memory is committed.','Assume all ten allocations succeed under the current limits and heuristic. They request 80 GiB, far more than the available backing.','Stated assumption not guaranteed.')
e('After about 16 GiB of pages have been touched (less, given the kernel and other processes), there is nothing left to reclaim and the OOM killer runs.','If those pages must remain backed and reclaim cannot satisfy demand, allocation can fail or trigger OOM handling before the requested 80 GiB is populated. Other memory consumers and policy determine when and what happens.','No deterministic kill threshold.',oom)
e('thread stacks reserved at 8 MiB','large mostly unused thread-stack reservations','Stack default varies.')
e('A 20 GiB process that forks shares every page copy-on-write, but in strict accounting the child must be charged another 20 GiB in case it writes everything.','An ordinary fork can share private writable data through copy-on-write. Strict accounting reserves for eligible writable private mappings that the child could copy; it does not simply duplicate VSZ or RSS, and special mappings have exceptions.','Commit notprocesssize/everypage.')
e('CommitLimit = swap\n            + RAM * overcommit_ratio / 100','CommitLimit = swap + usable_RAM\n              * overcommit_ratio / 100','Huge reservation subtractionexplicit.')
e('`vm.overcommit_ratio` defaults to 50 (huge-page reservations are subtracted from RAM first).','Here usable_RAM excludes reserved HugeTLB pages. The ratio defaults to 50; a nonzero `vm.overcommit_kbytes` supplies an absolute RAM contribution instead. Per-user/admin reserves can further restrict admission.','Absolute override/reserves omitted.')
e('64 GiB of RAM, 8 GiB of swap, ratio 50.','64 GiB of RAM, 8 GiB of swap, ratio 50, no HugeTLB reservation and overcommit_kbytes zero.','Formula assumptions.')
e('Strict mode makes failure visible as `malloc` returning NULL, which robust programs can handle, but it wastes RAM unless the ratio is tuned, and it breaks large forks. Redis\'s documentation recommends `vm.overcommit_memory = 1` precisely so that its snapshot `fork` cannot fail.','Strict admission can make insufficient commitment visible as an allocation error, but does not rule out cgroup OOM or every later failure. RAM outside the commit budget is not necessarily wasted: file-backed cache and kernel users can use it. Large forks can fail admission. Redis recommends mode 1 to avoid this particular overcommit rejection; fork can still fail for other reasons and COW still needs real headroom.','Strict not waste or universalguarantee.',oc+re)
e('When an allocation (usually a page fault) cannot be satisfied even after reclaim and compaction, the kernel invokes the **OOM killer**. It scores every eligible process and sends the highest scorer `SIGKILL`, which cannot be caught.','Some allocation failures invoke the **OOM killer** after permitted reclaim paths are exhausted. Others return failure, retry or follow configured panic policy. The ordinary victim-selection path compares eligible tasks within the relevant scope and sends SIGKILL; special cases such as killing the allocating task or an existing exiting victim can change the path.','OOM allocation flags and policyexceptions.',oom)
e('Each unit is worth one-thousandth of total memory (RAM plus swap), so +500 makes a process look as if it used an extra half of RAM.','In the simplified score, each unit adds one-thousandth of the applicable memory allowance; scope can be system-wide, cpuset/NUMA constrained or cgroup limited. +500 adds half that allowance, not necessarily half physical RAM.','Applicable total notglobalRAM.',oom)
e('systemd and sshd often use negative values so you can still log in.','Service policies can set negative values to influence relative selection; verify the actual unit and process settings.','Unverified defaults removed.',oom)
e('The default heuristic simply kills the biggest process, which is often your main service, not the misbehaving cron job. Because the kernel waits until reclaim has truly failed, the machine may thrash for minutes first. That is why user-space killers such as **systemd-oomd** and **earlyoom** watch PSI or free memory and act earlier, choosing whole cgroups.','Badness is not simply largest RSS: swap, page tables, adjustments and eligibility matter. User-space tools can act on pressure before kernel OOM. **systemd-oomd** can target cgroups using configured pressure policy; **earlyoom** normally selects processes using available-memory/swap thresholds and process scores. Neither guarantees an earlier kill in every workload.','Earlyoom notwholecgroups;no worstprocess/rate.',oom+['https://github.com/rfjakob/earlyoom'])
e('| USS | Pages only this process uses |','| USS | Resident pages reported as private to the process |','Resident qualification.',sm)
e('Summing PSS gives a true total.','Summing PSS over all relevant sharers approximates the resident pages represented by those mappings; it excludes unmapped cache and other kernel memory and has accounting/rounding caveats.','Not systemtrue total.',sm)
e('- **USS** is what you would get back by killing the process.','- **USS** is private resident memory, not a promise of how much RAM becomes free on exit. Private file-backed cache can remain cached, and kernel resources released on exit are not all in USS.','USSnot guaranteed freedmemory.',sm)
e('share 100 MiB of copy-on-write pages from the master.','share 100 MiB of resident copy-on-write pages. Assume only these four workers still map them; the master has exited or unmapped that region.','Master is fifth sharer otherwise.',sm)
e('RssAnon** (heap, stack: needs RAM or swap), **RssFile** (mapped files: can be dropped and reread)','RssAnon** (resident anonymous mappings, including heap and stack), **RssFile** (resident file mappings; clean reclaimable pages may be dropped and reread)','Filepagesdirty/pinned caveat.',sm)
e('Its memory controller charges every page a group\'s processes allocate, including **page cache** they cause, to that group.','The memory controller accounts for anonymous memory, file cache and many kernel allocations. Shared pages normally have one owning cgroup charge rather than a full charge to every accessor; accounting scope and special memory types have exceptions.','Not everypageallaccessors.',cg)
e('Nothing is killed. This is the gentle limit.','Crossing high does not itself invoke the kernel OOM killer. Independent global/ancestor pressure or a user-space OOM policy can still kill tasks.','Not high implies nokill anywhere.',cg)
e('allocations trigger reclaim within the group (dropping its cache first). If that fails, the **OOM killer runs inside the cgroup only** and kills one of its processes, or the whole group if `memory.oom.group` is set.','charged allocations may trigger reclaim within the constrained subtree. If the limit cannot be met, eligible allocation paths can invoke cgroup OOM; some allocations instead fail. Victims stay within the constrained subtree. `memory.oom.group` requests group killing, with exceptions including tasks protected by oom_score_adj=-1000.','Not alwayscachefirst/allkill.',cg)
e('A container that streams a 50 GiB file will see `memory.current` climb to its limit as the page cache fills. That is normally fine, because cache is reclaimed before any OOM kill.','A container reading a large file can accumulate a page-cache charge, depending on ownership, readahead and reclaim. Clean unpinned cache is reclaimable, but the file counter alone does not prove that usage is safe or all reclaim can succeed.','No deterministic reacheslimit/cachealwayssafe.',cg)
e('When a container hits its limit, the kernel\'s cgroup OOM killer kills a process in it. Kubernetes reports **OOMKilled** with exit code **137** (128 + 9, SIGKILL). This is separate from **kubelet eviction**, where the node itself runs low and kubelet evicts whole pods, starting with those using most above their requests.','A limit-related OOM can kill a container process, commonly reported as **OOMKilled** with code **137** (the usual 128+SIGKILL convention). Code 137 alone does not prove OOM, and OOMKilled alone does not prove the container\'s own limit was the cause: node/global OOM is possible. **Kubelet eviction** is separate; memory-pressure ranking considers whether usage exceeds requests, Pod Priority, and usage relative to requests.','Statusnotunique localOOM;evictionpriority.',ku)
e('- **JVM**: container-aware since JDK 10 (and 8u191). The default heap is 25% of the limit (`-XX:MaxRAMPercentage`). Setting the heap equal to the limit is a classic mistake, because metaspace, thread stacks, JIT code and direct buffers live outside the heap and push the total over.','- **JVM**: supported HotSpot versions can use container limits in heap ergonomics, but cgroup-version support, vendor/version and flags matter. MaxRAMPercentage commonly defaults to 25, with small-memory and other ergonomics affecting actual heap sizing. Metaspace, stacks, JIT, GC structures and native/direct buffers need additional room. Verify the running JVM\'s detected limit and flags.','Not allJDK8cgroupv2/25exact.',ja)
e('makes the garbage collector work harder as the process approaches a soft limit.','sets a soft budget for memory managed by the Go runtime; it excludes some external memory and may be exceeded to avoid excessive GC.','Not fullprocesshardlimit.',['https://go.dev/doc/gc-guide'])
e('`--max-old-space-size` caps the V8 heap.','`--max-old-space-size` limits V8 old-space, not the entire heap or total process RSS.','Oldspace !=entireheap.',['https://nodejs.org/api/cli.html#--max-old-space-sizesize-in-mib'])
e('A sensible rule: leave 20 to 30% headroom between a runtime\'s heap cap and the container limit.','Budget measured non-heap peaks, cache and allocation bursts below the container limit. A fixed 20–30% margin is not a guarantee for every runtime or workload.','No universal safepercentage.')
e('The signature is RSS (or PSS) that rises steadily with work done and never plateaus.','Persistent RSS/PSS growth can motivate investigation but neither proves a leak nor catches every leak.','Diagnosticfalse universal.',sm)
e('| Tool | Language | Cost |\n|---|---|---|\n| Valgrind memcheck | C/C++ | 20 to 30× slower |\n| AddressSanitizer + LSan | C/C++/Rust | about 2× |\n| heaptrack, bcc `memleak` | native | low, attachable |\n| jemalloc profiling | native | low, production |\n| `tracemalloc` | Python | moderate |','| Tool | Scope | Requirement |\n|---|---|---|\n| Valgrind Memcheck | Native memory checks | Supported platform, instrumented execution |\n| ASan + LSan | Native memory errors / unreachable allocations | Supported toolchain, build instrumentation |\n| heaptrack / bcc memleak | Native allocation history / outstanding allocations | Compatible hooks and permissions |\n| jemalloc profiling | Sampled allocator stacks | Profiling-enabled allocator configuration |\n| tracemalloc | Traced Python allocations | Enable before allocations of interest |\n\n> [!note] Measurement gap\n> No representative overhead measurements accompany this lesson. Fixed slowdown factors and claims that a tool is automatically safe for production are omitted. Benchmark the selected configuration and sampling rate.','Unsupported measurement ranges removedexplicit.',ls+bc)
e('Valgrind needs no recompile but is far slower. `memleak` from bcc uses eBPF to sample allocations in a running process that you cannot restart.','Valgrind generally needs no rebuild for instrumentation, though debug information improves reports. bcc memleak can attach to supported allocation functions, recording outstanding allocations observed after attachment; it does not by itself determine reachability or reconstruct all earlier allocations.','memleak not reachability/earlierhistory.',bc)
e('# leak.py:6: size=9806 KiB (+9806 KiB),\n# count=1001 (+1001), average=10032 B','# Output varies with Python and platform.','Outputrecordmissing/impldependent.',['https://docs.python.org/3/library/tracemalloc.html'])
e('1,000 calls × 10,000 bytes is about 9.8 MiB, all attributed to line 6.','The payload is 10,000,000 bytes, about **9.54 MiB** (9,765.625 KiB), plus object and list overhead. Snapshot attribution and exact counts vary by Python version and allocator.','WrongMiB arithmetic.', ['https://docs.python.org/3/library/tracemalloc.html'])
e('`RssAnon` growth is heap; `RssFile` or cgroup `file` growth is cache and usually harmless.','RssAnon includes anonymous mappings beyond the heap. RssFile and cgroup file counters cover different scopes; inspect dirty, writeback, shmem, reclaim and pressure before calling growth harmless.','Countersnot proof heap/harmless.',cg+sm)
e('- Linux overcommits by default: malloc succeeds on address space, and failure arrives later as an OOM kill; mode 2 enforces CommitLimit = swap + RAM × ratio / 100.','- Linux defaults to heuristic overcommit; allocation may fail immediately or later. Strict commit accounting uses ratio/absolute-budget settings and applicable reservations.','Summary no allmalloc succeeds.')
e('- The OOM killer SIGKILLs the process with the highest badness (RSS, swap and page tables, biased by oom_score_adj from −1000 to +1000) and logs it in dmesg.','- Ordinary OOM selection scores eligible tasks in its scope using resident, swapped and page-table usage plus adjustment; context and policies add exceptions.','Summaryscope.',oom)
e('PSS splits them fairly and sums correctly, USS is what killing the process frees','PSS apportions shared resident mappings, USS measures private resident pages rather than guaranteed exit savings','SummaryUSS.',sm)
e('Kubernetes limits map to memory.max and kills show as exit 137.','Kubernetes cgroup-v2 limits commonly map to memory.max; exit 137 alone does not identify OOM.','Summarynotunique.',ku)
f(1,'text','About 1.2 GiB is resident; the remaining virtual space may be untouched, swapped out or nonresident file mappings','Nonresident not necessarilyemptyreservations.',sm,o=1)
f(1,'explanation','Correct. Address-space reservation is not resident payload, but metadata and some commitment costs can exist before touching it. These numbers alone do not establish swap use.','Not free/noSwapinference.',sm,o=1)
f(1,'explanation','Virtual mappings can exceed RAM. The available address range depends on architecture, paging mode and operating-system policy, not universally 128 TiB.','5level.',sm,o=2)
f(1,'explanation','RSS includes shared pages; private resident accounting also does not exactly predict exit savings, since cache can remain and kernel resources may be released.','USSnot freed.',sm,o=3)
f(1,'workedExample','VSZ describes mapped virtual address ranges, while RSS counts resident mappings. JVM reservations, file mappings and allocator heaps can contribute to VSZ. A large gap may include untouched, swapped or nonresident file-backed pages; these two values cannot distinguish them. Inspect smaps and swap counters. RSS counts shared resident pages in each mapping process; PSS apportions that sharing, while USS describes private resident pages.','Fix entire diagnostic model.',sm)
f(2,'text','A process was reported killed for an OOM condition; code 137 is consistent with SIGKILL, but logs are needed to distinguish cgroup-limit from node/global OOM','OOMKillednot alwaysown limit.',ku,o=2)
f(2,'explanation','Correct. The reason reports OOM and the code is consistent with SIGKILL. Check kernel, cgroup and node evidence to determine the scope.','Scopes.',ku,o=2)
f(2,'workedExample','137 is conventionally 128+9 (SIGKILL), but the number alone is not proof of OOM. OOMKilled supplies the runtime-reported reason. Inspect kernel logs, memory.events and node pressure to distinguish a container/ancestor limit from global OOM. A local cgroup OOM can occur with free host RAM. Then size limits and demand from measured peaks, rather than assuming every OOMKilled means the leaf container crossed its own limit.','No unique leaflimit inference.',ku+cg)
f(3,'prompt','On a 16 GiB host with no swap and vm.overcommit_memory=0, assume ten 8 GiB allocations all succeed. The program then writes distinct data into all requested pages. Which risk does this illustrate?','Allsuccessgiven not guaranteed.')
f(3,'text','Successful reservations do not guarantee backing; sustained demand can exhaust reclaimable memory and trigger allocation failure or OOM handling','Not definitewhich kill.',o=3)
f(3,'explanation','Correct. Heuristic admission can permit more commitment than available backing. Runtime limits and other consumers determine the later outcome.','No allsuccess implication.',o=3)
f(3,'explanation','Mode 0 does not enforce a simple running RAM-size cutoff; in this scenario success is an explicit assumption. Other limits could cause immediate failure in another run.','No eachrequestguaranteed.',o=0)
f(3,'explanation','Compression is configuration dependent and data dependent; the stated no-swap model does not supply enough backing merely because virtual allocations succeeded.','Distinctdata cancompress notalwaysimpossible.',o=1)
f(3,'workedExample','Ten successful 8 GiB requests total 80 GiB of requested space, not 80 GiB of resident data. Filling it needs backing unless data can be reclaimed/discarded. Under the stated no-swap assumption, ordinary live anonymous contents cannot simply be dropped. Pressure can lead to failed allocations or OOM action. Mode 0 still permits immediate malloc failures in other circumstances; callers must check results.','Remove nullchecknever false.')
f(4,'prompt',json.loads(Path(q).read_text())[3]['prompt'].replace('no huge pages reserved','no huge pages reserved; overcommit_kbytes=0'),'Absolute override excluded.')
f(4,'workedExample','With the stated ratio model, CommitLimit is 4+32×0.8=29.6 GiB (subject to page-unit rounding). Strict admission checks eligible new commitment and applicable reserves; not every mmap or malloc creates an equal new charge. At ratio 50 the budget is 20 GiB. RAM outside that commitment budget can still serve file cache and kernel users, so it is not necessarily wasted.','Notallalloc/unusedRAM.')
f(5,'prompt',json.loads(Path(q).read_text())[4]['prompt'].replace('(count only the workers)','(assume these eight workers are the only remaining mappings of those shared pages, and all stated pages are resident)'),'Master excludedmustunmap not justomitcount.',sm)
f(6,'explanation','Correct. Exceeding high does not itself invoke the kernel OOM killer, although global/ancestor OOM or a user-space pressure policy may independently kill tasks.','Notonlymaxkills.',cg,o=0)
f(6,'text','When an eligible allocation triggers a cgroup OOM, victim selection is confined to the constrained subtree','Notallfailurekill.',cg,o=1)
f(6,'explanation','Correct. Local scope can include descendant cgroups and can run out of budget even with free host RAM. Some allocation failures do not invoke OOM.','Subtree.',cg,o=1)
f(6,'text','New page-cache pages allocated on behalf of a group can be charged to its memory.current','Not everyread moves existingcharge.',cg,o=2)
f(6,'explanation','Correct. Shared cache normally has one owning charge; merely reading an already-charged shared page does not charge its full size again to every reader.','Shared ownership.',cg,o=2)
f(6,'explanation','Incorrect. memory.min provides hard reclaim protection within the effective hierarchical boundary; overcommitted protection is apportioned, not an unlimited absolute guarantee.','Effective hierarchy.',cg,o=3)
f(6,'workedExample','memory.max bounds charged usage with reclaim and possible local OOM; memory.high applies reclaim/throttling without itself invoking OOM; min/low provide hard/best-effort protection within effective hierarchical budgets. memory.current includes charged file cache and kernel memory. memory.events counters have specific semantics and are hierarchical by default; they are not simply one count per threshold crossing. Inspect memory.events.local when appropriate and correlate oom/oom_kill with logs.','Counter semantics notthresholdcrossings.',cg)
f(7,'prompt',json.loads(Path(q).read_text())[6]['prompt']+' Assume only A and B are eligible, the applicable allowance is 16 GiB, and ordinary highest-badness selection is used.','Scope othercandidates/policies.',oom)
f(7,'explanation','The ordinary path selects a victim; related threads or tasks sharing its address space can also be killed, and cgroup group-kill policy adds cases. It does not simply kill both listed processes in RSS order.','Sharedmmmultiple evenwithoutgroup.',oom,o=2)
f(8,'prompt',json.loads(Path(q).read_text())[7]['prompt']+' Further inspection confirms the file usage is mostly clean, unpinned disk-backed cache and current pressure is low.','File includesdirty/tmpfs missingassumption.',cg)
f(8,'text','Mostly clean disk-backed cache is charged to the group and can be reclaimed; this can be normal, but monitor reclaim pressure and other memory consumers','Not guaranteedhealthy.',cg,o=1)
f(8,'explanation','The additional evidence makes immediately raising the limit unjustified; near-limit usage alone does not establish imminent OOM. Reclaim can still have workload costs.','Not alwayssafe.',cg,o=0)
f(8,'explanation','Correct under the added cache-state evidence. No single counter proves ongoing safety, and anonymous pages are not the only possible unreclaimable usage.','Scope.',cg,o=1)
f(8,'explanation','The file counter alone does not prove an application leak, but application bugs can grow tmpfs, mappings or dirty files. The additional clean-cache evidence supports ordinary caching here.','Leaksnotonlyanon.',cg,o=2)
f(8,'workedExample','140 MiB anon + 860 MiB file leaves about 20 MiB of other charged usage in the stated 1020 MiB total. The added evidence identifies mostly clean disk-backed cache, which can be reclaimed. File also includes categories such as tmpfs and dirty/writeback pages, so file usage is not automatically safe. OOM can result from several charged categories and reclaim restrictions, not only anon approaching the limit. Monitor pressure, reclaim, events and workload latency alongside usage.','NoonlyanonOOM/instantlydrop.',cg)
f(9,'prompt',json.loads(Path(q).read_text())[8]['prompt'].replace('Why?','Which explanation is consistent with these observations and should be investigated?'),'Not diagnosticproof.',ja+cg)
f(9,'text','Non-heap and other cgroup charges can push the total above 4 GiB even while the Java heap stays below its cap','Hypothesiscorrect.',ja+cg,o=3)
f(9,'explanation','Correct. Measure non-heap and other charged memory and leave adequate room; a fixed percentage is not a universally safe value.','No magic75%.',ja,o=3)
f(9,'workedExample','Xmx limits the Java heap, while cgroup accounting also includes native allocations, stacks, code, GC structures and charged file/kernel memory. Measure actual peaks and verify the JVM detects the container budget. Native Memory Tracking can show HotSpot categories, but is not complete accounting of every third-party native allocation or cgroup charge. Reduce the heap target or increase the measured total budget as appropriate. Exact metaspace/stack/code-cache sizes and a universally safe heap percentage are omitted because they depend on build, flags and workload.','Unverifiedtypical exactnumbers visiblyomit.',ja)
f(10,'prompt',json.loads(Path(q).read_text())[9]['prompt']+' Assume overcommit_kbytes=0, no HugeTLB reservation, and diagnostics confirm a 24 GiB existing eligible commit plus another 24 GiB required by fork.','Datasetnotcommit exactstateprovided.')
f(10,'explanation','Ordinary fork shares much private memory through COW initially, but still needs metadata and can later need substantial backing. The confirmed failure here is admission, not a full immediate payload copy.','Not physicalmemoryruledoutuniversally.',o=0)
f(10,'explanation','THP does not categorically prohibit fork. Fork can still fail for other resource reasons; the stated diagnostics identify commit admission.','THPENOMEMnotimpossible.',o=2)
f(10,'explanation','oom_score is for victim selection, not the commit-admission calculation. ENOMEM from fork can have several causes; this scenario supplies evidence for the commit check.','ENOMEMnotonlycommit.',o=3)
f(10,'workedExample','Under the stated accounting assumptions, CommitLimit=64×0.5=32 GiB and the proposed total is 24+24=48 GiB, so strict admission rejects it. Redis recommends mode 1 to avoid this check, but that does not guarantee fork success or enough later COW memory. Increasing the strict budget sufficiently is another policy choice. Measure actual commit, resident and peak COW demand; a few-GiB overhead or sufficient headroom cannot be inferred from dataset size alone.','No guaranteedforkor inventedfewGiB.',oc+re)
f(11,'explanation','Correct. Compare live, active/resident and retained metrics. Retention can motivate allocator tuning or application lifetime/layout changes; it does not imply code changes are never useful.','Falseonlytuning.',o=0)
f(11,'text','On supported Linux toolchains with leak detection enabled, ASan can integrate LSan to report unreachable allocations at exit','Platformconfigscope.',ls,o=1)
f(11,'explanation','Correct. Availability and defaults depend on platform/toolchain/options. Measure staging overhead rather than assuming a universal factor.','No fixed2x.',ls,o=1)
f(11,'explanation','Correct where probes, allocator symbols and permissions permit. It observes allocations after attachment and outstanding does not mean unreachable; measure probe overhead.','Not automaticsafeproduction.',bc,o=2)
f(11,'explanation','Incorrect. Memcheck can incur substantial instrumentation overhead. Lack of recompilation does not imply low cost or make it automatically suitable for a production instance.','Removefixed20-30ranking.',ls,o=3)
f(11,'workedExample','First compare allocator live/active/resident/retained metrics with OS accounting. LSan uses reachability; bcc memleak tracks observed allocation/free pairs and can include useful reachable allocations while missing pre-attachment allocations. A heap profiler can expose growing reachable structures by stack/site. Tool availability, coverage and overhead vary; no measured production slowdown or prevalence ranking is supplied here.','Unsubstantiatedmostleaks/remove memleakreachability.',ls+bc)
f(12,'prompt',json.loads(Path(q).read_text())[11]['prompt'].replace('What happens?','Assume all pages hold live data, charges belong to this cgroup, no earlier volume quota fails, and disk writeback can make progress. What is the key memory-budget difference?'),'AvoidautomaticoutcomeENOSPC/diskpressure.',cg)
f(12,'text','Disk-backed pages can be written back and reclaimed; 800 MiB of live anonymous data plus 1.5 GiB of live unswapped tmpfs exceeds the 2 GiB budget','Potentialkillnotcertain.',cg,o=2)
f(12,'explanation','Correct. 800+1536=2336 MiB, or 2.28125 GiB, before other charges. This cannot all remain charged within a 2048 MiB cap; allocation failure or OOM handling is possible.','Math/notalltmpfsfailurekill.',cg,o=2)
f(12,'workedExample','Disk-backed dirty pages can become clean through successful writeback and then be reclaimed. Live tmpfs contents cannot be discarded without losing file data; with no swap they consume memory while retained. The stated payload is 800+1536=2336 MiB=2.28125 GiB, above 2048 MiB before other charges. Depending on allocation path and limits, failure can appear as OOM handling or an error; disk-backed case also needs room for overhead and functioning reclaim/writeback. Budget memory-backed volumes explicitly.','Avoid fine/automatic137.',cg)
